===Major party campaigns===

====Progressive Party (PPO)====
[[File:Honda Shigetsuma.png|thumb|left|150px|Prime Minister Honda Shigetsuma campaigning in his home district of Noto.]]
Prime Minister [[Honda Shigetsuma]] sought to frame the election as a choice between "responsible governance" and "populist chaos." The PPO campaign emphasized Honda's record of passing the [[National Universal Health Act]] and navigating the country through multiple crises since 2021. Honda argued that his administration's "Calculated Concession" approach—including the "15% Energy Rebate" funded by a windfall levy on major corporations—demonstrated his ability to govern pragmatically.

However, the PPO faced significant headwinds. Honda's defense of one-man train operations during the March Maelstrom made him deeply unpopular in urban districts. His campaign was forced to retreat to his traditional rural strongholds in [[Noto Prefecture]] and [[Kiso Prefecture]], where his personal popularity and family dynasty remain potent. Honda attempted to paint both the LDP and OSP as "extremes" that would either dismantle the welfare state (LDP) or bankrupt the nation (OSP).

Critics noted that the PPO's campaign lacked energy compared to 2021. Honda, a Wharton-educated aristocrat from one of Okaiken's most prominent political dynasties, struggled to connect with voters angered by the rail crisis and rising living costs. The party's internal polling reportedly showed Honda's approval ratings at historic lows for an incumbent prime minister.

====Liberal Democratic Party (LDP)====
[[File:Sadanuma_Koichiro.png|thumb|right|150px|LDP leader Koichiro Sadanuma delivering a policy speech in Akita.]]
LDP leader [[Koichiro Sadanuma]] positioned himself as the "adult in the room," deliberately contrasting his technocratic stoicism with what he termed Honda's "aristocratic smugness" and Nakayama's "populist theatrics." The LDP campaign focused relentlessly on the themes of "Restoring Competence" and "Restoring the Standard of Safety."

Sadanuma, a former Ministry of Finance bureaucrat known as a "fiscal hawk," pledged to conduct a total safety audit of [[Okaiken Railways]] and establish an emergency stabilization fund for the Sanese En. He attacked both the PPO and OSP, arguing that the hung parliament produced a "Frankenstein government" incapable of coherent policy-making. The LDP proposed a "Zero-Based Budgeting" approach, demanding that every government program justify its existence anew.

The LDP's strategy was to capture the center-ground voters who prioritize a return to order. Sadanuma carefully avoided the inflammatory rhetoric regarding immigration that characterized the party's right wing under former leader [[Hakuya Terakubo]], focusing instead on the economic necessity of skilled labor while emphasizing strict border enforcement. This moderate positioning allowed the LDP to appeal to business conservatives and rural voters disillusioned with the PPO.

However, Sadanuma faced a significant challenge in countering Nakayama's surging personal popularity. The LDP leader frequently dismissed Nakayama's resignation from the cabinet as "theatrical abandonment" of his ministerial duties during a national emergency—a line of attack that had limited success with younger voters.

====Okaiken Socialist Party (OSP)====
[[File:Rento Nakayama 2.png|thumb|left|150px|OSP leader Rento Nakayama at a campaign rally in Okami.]]
OSP leader [[Rento Nakayama]] emerged as the breakout star of the campaign, capitalizing on his handling of the March Maelstrom to position himself as the authentic voice of the working class. Nakayama's campaign expanded from railway safety to a broader "{{Nihongo|Wellness First|健康第一|Kenkō Daiichi}}" economic platform, targeting the high cost of living, mental health services, and the "sick society" that prioritizes profit over well-being.

Nakayama's unique background as a former actor, bodybuilder, and fitness influencer allowed him to bypass traditional media entirely. His campaign was run primarily through viral videos on TikTok and Instagram, where he spoke directly to voters in a casual, unscripted-feeling style. His rallies, which often began with him doing push-ups on stage, continued to attract thousands of young, previously disengaged voters in a phenomenon the media dubbed the second wave of "Nakayama-mania."

The centerpiece of the OSP platform was the "Two-Man Mandate"—a legislative proposal requiring two-person crews on all passenger rail operations in Okaiken. Nakayama framed this as a moral necessity, arguing that "a government that asks a student to die for a timetable is a government that has lost its soul." The OSP also pledged to implement the 5 trillion En "Inflation Relief Package" that Nakayama demanded during his time in cabinet, funded by deficit spending and new taxes on corporations.

Critics attacked Nakayama's inexperience and economic policies. LDP leader Sadanuma labeled him "economically illiterate," while the SCP attempted to outflank the OSP from the left, arguing that Nakayama's willingness to enter coalition with the PPO in 2025 made him complicit in Honda's failures. Nakayama dismissed these attacks as "noise from people who don't do the work."

====Sanese Communist Party (SCP)====
[[File:Kousuke Ikazaki.png|thumb|left|150px|SCP leader Kousuke Ikazaki]]

The [[Sanese Communist Party]] experienced a dramatic turnaround following the ouster of leader [[Shintaro Hamabe]] on April 27, 2026. Hamabe, who had led the party to its disastrous 2025 result and lost his own seat, was removed in a unanimous vote of the party Central Committee after internal polling showed the SCP facing potential extinction.

Hamabe's replacement is [[Kousuke Ikazaki]], the former Minister of Defence under Prime Minister [[Sho Aoyagi]] (2016–2021). Unlike Hamabe, whose academic Marxism and "capitalist parasite" gaffes alienated voters, Ikazaki is a cold, calculating political operator with a reputation for surgical precision. Described by political analysts as "terrifyingly competent," Ikazaki possesses an intimidating presence and a strategic mind honed during his tenure managing Okaiken's defense portfolio during the volatile post-Kurosawa period.

Ikazaki's leadership style was characterized by ruthless efficiency and an uncompromising demand for party discipline. He reportedly purged several Hamabe loyalists from key party positions and instituted a "war footing" campaign structure. His public demeanor—measured, intense, and devoid of the warmth typical of Sanese politicians—drew comparisons to historical wartime leaders. One [[KTK News]] analyst described his first press conference as "watching a predator enter a room full of prey."

The SCP's new messaging under Ikazaki was markedly more aggressive. Rather than attacking Nakayama as a "populist opportunist," Ikazaki framed the OSP leader as fundamentally unprepared for the responsibilities of governance. In his first major speech on April 29, Ikazaki stated: "Charisma without competence is not leadership—it is a performance. The working class deserves more than a fitness influencer who discovered politics six months ago."

This precision-strike approach began to yield results. Internal polling suggested that Ikazaki's "strength through discipline" message resonated with older left-wing voters who were skeptical of Nakayama's social media-driven campaign. The SCP also successfully framed itself as the "adult left"—experienced, doctrinally consistent, and capable of actual governance rather than performative opposition.

The centerpiece of the SCP platform remained the total nationalization of rail safety and the "Parasite Tax" on wealth over 500 million En, but Ikazaki added proposals for a complete restructuring of Okaiken's security apparatus and expanded veteran services, drawing on his defense background. His aggressive criticism of Honda's rail policies also pulled the OSP toward more radical positions, threatening to split the left-wing vote.

=== Debates ===
==== First leaders' debate (30 April) ====
{{Main|Okaiken first leaders' debate, 2026}}
[[File:2026 First Leaders Debate.png|thumb|left|350px|The First Leader's debate, hosted by KTK]]
The first televised leaders' debate was held on 30 April 2026, hosted by KTK at their Okami headquarters. Breaking from the traditional, cluttered aesthetic of Sanese election broadcasts, KTK utilized a minimalist studio featuring flat graphics and a wide-angle layout that prioritized candidates' physical presence.

The debate was moderated by Ryuki Fukumoto and focused primarily on the March Maelstrom—the civil unrest following the Nakayama Station dragging incident—and the growing distrust of the direction of the Sanese Economy as a result of the En Crisis. The atmosphere was described by the Okami Times as "combustible," with the PPO-OSP coalition having functionally collapsed only weeks prior.

===== Candidate performance and notable gaffes =====
While Kousuke Ikazaki (SCP) was widely praised for a terrifyingly disciplined performance, adhering to his "strength through discipline" messaging without error, the other three candidates suffered significant rhetorical lapses that dominated the following morning's headlines.

* Shigetsuma Honda (PPO): The Prime Minister appeared detached and overly technocratic. When questioned by a relative of the Nakayama Station victim regarding rail safety, Honda referred to the incident as "an unfortunate friction in the optimization of the national grid." The term "friction" (摩擦 - masatsu) was immediately seized upon by the Mirror Yoshi movement as proof of Honda's "Modernization over Mortality" agenda being inherently inhumane.
* Koichiro Sadanuma (LDP): In an attempt to appeal to fiscal conservatives, Sadanuma blundered while discussing the return to manual safety protocols. He described the traditional Point-and-Call (shisakanko) method as "obsolete performance art" that hindered the national GDP. This gaffe alienated the LDP's core rural base and senior voters, who viewed the comment as an insult to Sanese labor culture and safety traditions.
* Rento Nakayama (OSP): Maintaining his "Alpha" influencer persona, Nakayama struggled to pivot to the gravity of the national crisis. During a segment on mental health and the March strikes, he suggested that the striking rail workers simply needed "more mental fortitude and a rigorous self-improvement regimen" to overcome their grievances. Critics labeled the remark "Digital Narcissism," suggesting Nakayama viewed national policy through the lens of a fitness influencer rather than a statesman.

