===== Reaction =====
Instant "thermometer" polls conducted by KTK immediately following the broadcast showed a sharp divide. While Honda maintained a slim lead among those over 50, Nakayama dominated the 18–30 demographic. However, the most significant shift was the "Ikazaki Surge"; despite the SCP's historically low polling, Ikazaki's lack of gaffes and cold, military-style precision resulted in a 4% bump in "preferred Prime Minister" metrics overnight.

[[Kousuke Ikazaki]] (SCP) was universally assessed as the debate's winner, producing a physical folder of procurement documents exposing the WellnessConnect scheme, naming the Yamamoto Hina Safety Commission, and delivering what the ''Okami Times'' described as "a masterclass in opposition politics." Post-debate polling showed the SCP surging from 7.2% to 11.8%.

==== Second leaders' debate (10 May) ====
{{Main|Okaiken second leaders' debate, 2026}}
[[File:Second Leaders Debate 2026.png|thumb|right|350px|The second leaders' debate, broadcast jointly from the Okami National Broadcast Centre.]]
The second televised leaders' debate was held on May 10, 2026, jointly broadcast by KTK, OR News, and Asahi Okaiken from the Okami National Broadcast Centre. The debate occurred in the immediate aftermath of the [[Satori AI Blackout Crisis]] and the historic [[ACTA credit rating|ACTA downgrade]] of Okaiken's sovereign credit rating from 5 stars to 3 stars—the first such downgrade in the agency's post-war history.

The debate was moderated by '''Aiko Hasegawa''' of Asahi Okaiken, the only senior female political moderator in Sanese broadcast journalism. The format was expanded from the first debate to include all seven party leaders across six segments: The ACTA Downgrade and Economic Recovery; The Satori Blackout and Digital Infrastructure; Rail Safety and the Two-Man Mandate; Social Welfare and the Cost of Living; Foreign Policy and Regional Security; and Closing Statements.

===== Onizuka's document revelation =====
In a pivotal moment during Segment Two, NKP leader [[Soichiro Onizuka]] produced internal Ministry of Digital Affairs communications from 2024 showing that ministry officials had raised concerns about the Satori grid's disaster recovery protocols and recommended a delay in full integration. Onizuka revealed that these recommendations were overruled by the Prime Minister's office, with a directive signed by Chief Cabinet Secretary Hayashi explicitly ordering the integration to proceed due to "efficiency targets for the fiscal year."

Honda defended the decision, stating that subsequent communications confirmed safeguards were in place and an independent audit had approved the integration. Onizuka countered that the audit was conducted by a firm receiving 40% of its revenue from Kizan Systems contracts, with the lead auditor having served on Kizan's advisory board until 2022—a conflict of interest "concealed by procedural formality."

===== Yamamoto Kenji's question =====
During Segment Three on rail safety, Yamamoto Kenji—a man whose wife Hanako Yamamoto died in a train fire on the Toyozawa Line—directly addressed Honda: "Prime Minister, you have said that the Two-Man Mandate is not cost-effective... if a second crew member had been on that train, my wife might be alive today. Is her life worth 120 billion En?"

Honda responded without technical justification: "Mr. Yamamoto, I cannot give you an answer that will satisfy the loss you have experienced... every decision a government makes involves trade-offs between competing goods... I am sorry for your loss. I am sorry that the systems we built were not sufficient to prevent it." He concluded with a deep bow—an acknowledgment that could not, politically, be an apology.

===== Nakayama's admission =====
In a moment that drew significant attention, OSP leader Rento Nakayama acknowledged his own complicity in the Satori crisis: "In 2024, when the Satori integration was being finalized, I sat in a Cabinet meeting. The Chief Cabinet Secretary presented the timeline. The Prime Minister endorsed it. And I said nothing." Nakayama stated he had concerns about disaster recovery protocols but remained silent due to "efficiency metrics, budget projections, and the political calendar." The admission was widely assessed as both politically damaging and politically redemptive.

===== Yoshida's question to Ikazaki =====
SDP leader Shimane Yoshida—the only woman on the debate stage—asked Ikazaki who would be inside the fortress when the doors closed, presenting statistical evidence that all major party proposed cabinets were 85% male. Her proposal of a 40% women's cabinet threshold was publicly accepted by every candidate on the stage before the broadcast ended.

===== Hasegawa's unscripted question =====
Before closing statements, moderator Aiko Hasegawa asked Ikazaki who was inside the man who would build the fortress—prompting his most personal public statement, referencing the thesis rejection letter he keeps in a desk drawer.

===== Honda's resignation announcement =====
Honda announced on live television that he would resign as PPO leader on May 18th regardless of the election result, directly addressing his father's "rulers by blood, not merely by ballot" philosophy for the first time in his public career.

===== The coalition signal =====
Nakayama explicitly signalled coalition readiness with Ikazaki on live television: "If the voters of Okaiken give us a mandate to rebuild this country together, I think we owe it to them to try." Ikazaki acknowledged the signal with characteristic precision.

