==Regional dynamics==

===Okami Block (OK-1)===
The [[Okami Metropolis]], with its 56 single-member district seats, was projected to be the site of the OSP's strongest performance. The party was expected to sweep nearly all urban seats, buoyed by the "Nakayama effect" and intense anger over the railway crisis. Dense wards like [[Mitsu-Minato]] and [[Hojo]], which were at the epicenter of the March Maelstrom protests, showed OSP support exceeding 40% in internal polling. The PPO was expected to lose most of its Okami seats, while the LDP focused its resources on suburban districts where Sadanuma's "Restoring Competence" message resonated with middle-class voters. In the actual result, the SCP outperformed projections, taking 22 of the 56 seats.

===Noto (OK-19) & Kiso (OK-18)===
These rural prefectures remained the final strongholds of the PPO. Honda Shigetsuma's personal popularity in [[Wajima]] and [[Nanao]]—where his family has been a political force for generations—held, but the party faced unprecedented challenges from LDP moderates who capitalized on rural resentment over the coalition's urban-focused policies. The OSP attempted to make inroads by emphasizing Nakayama's working-class background and attacking the "aristocratic" Honda dynasty, but these messages had limited success in areas where the Honda name carries significant weight.

===Aizawa (OK-13)===
A major battleground for the LDP. The party campaigned heavily on "Restoring Order" to the rural rail lines that were neglected during the coalition's urban-focused "Wellness" debates. Sadanuma visited the prefecture multiple times, promising infrastructure investment and accusing the PPO of taking rural voters for granted. The OSP also made inroads in working-class industrial districts, splitting the opposition vote and potentially allowing LDP candidates to win with pluralities.

===Yagikoshu (Southern Island)===
The southern prefectures, traditionally LDP strongholds, were expected to return to the party after a period of PPO gains in the 2021 election. The LDP's focus on fiscal responsibility and "traditional safety redundancy" resonated with older voters concerned about the stability of their rail connections to the capital.

==Analysis and potential outcomes==

===Potential coalitions===
Given current polling, no single party was projected to win an absolute majority of 238 seats. Political analysts outlined several potential coalition scenarios:

* '''LDP-OSP Coalition:''' Though ideologically divergent, Sadanuma and Nakayama were photographed in a high-stakes encounter in February 2026, leading to speculation about a "Grand Left-Right Alliance" against the PPO. Such a coalition would require significant compromises on both sides regarding fiscal policy and defense spending.

* '''LDP-PPO Grand Coalition:''' A theoretical "national unity" government combining the two largest centrist parties. This scenario was considered unlikely given the bitter personal rivalry between Honda and Sadanuma, and the LDP's attacks on the National Universal Health Act.

* '''OSP-led Coalition with SCP:''' If the OSP finished as the largest party, Nakayama could attempt to form a coalition government with support from the SCP under Kousuke Ikazaki. However, this scenario became complicated by Ikazaki's late-campaign attacks on Nakayama's "policy vagueness"—the new SCP leader signaled he would extract significant concessions before supporting any OSP-led government, potentially demanding the Defence portfolio and a hardline stance on railway safety legislation.<ref>{{cite news |title=Ikazaki's Price: What the SCP Would Demand |url= |work=''[[Toyozawa Shinbun]]'' |date=April 27, 2026}}</ref>

* '''PPO-LDP Coalition:''' A return to the traditional political order, with the PPO and LDP sharing power. This scenario was considered highly unlikely given the ideological gulf between the parties and the personal animosity between their leaders.

