Voter turnout: 74.2% ({{increase}} 5.4% from 2025)

The election night confirmed the most dramatic realignment in modern Sanese political history. The [[Progressive Party of Okaiken|PPO]] suffered a catastrophic collapse, losing 118 seats—more than any governing party in post-war history—to finish with just 63, a result that ended the party's decades-long dominance of Sanese politics. Outgoing Prime Minister Honda, who had announced his resignation as party leader during the second debate, called the result "a verdict I accept without reservation. The people have spoken, and they have said: enough."

The [[Sanese Communist Party|SCP]] achieved the most stunning upset of the night. Entering the election with just 45 seats and a leader who had been in post for only twenty days, the party surged to 115 seats—a gain of 70—on the strength of the "Ikazaki Effect" and the Satori Blackout's validation of the party's infrastructure positions. SCP leader [[Kousuke Ikazaki]] claimed victory at 23:41 on election night in what the ''Okami Times'' described as "the most unsentimental victory speech in modern Sanese political history," stating: "The fortress is built. Now we decide who lives inside it."

The [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|LDP]] saw its polling surge fade on election day, losing 18 seats to finish at 110 despite winning the popular vote with 26.1%. The party's support was concentrated in rural and southern strongholds, but it failed to make the urban inroads that Sadanuma's campaign had targeted. LDP leader [[Koichiro Sadanuma]] acknowledged the result as "a rejection of both the government and the opposition extremes," confirming the LDP would remain in opposition rather than enter coalition with either the SCP or OSP.

The [[Okaiken Socialist Party|OSP]] under [[Rento Nakayama]] doubled its representation from 50 to 100 seats, confirming the second wave of "Nakayama-mania" but falling short of the projections that had placed the party as high as 156 seats before the Satori Blackout shifted momentum toward the SCP. Nakayama struck a conciliatory tone, acknowledging his own complicity in the Satori decision and signalling openness to coalition with Ikazaki: "If the voters of Okaiken give us a mandate to rebuild this country together, I think we owe it to them to try."

The [[Social Democratic Party (Okaiken)|SDP]] was the quiet success story of the night, gaining 11 seats to reach 36—its best result since 2015—almost entirely on the strength of leader [[Shimane Yoshida]]'s debate performance and the "Yoshida Threshold" momentum that brought a wave of female candidates into the Diet. Yoshida declared the result "proof that the fortress needs windows, not just walls."

The smaller parties held steady. The [[New Komeito Party|NKP]] gained 2 seats to reach 32, while the [[Okaiken Innovation Party|OIP]] also gained 2 to reach 18. Both parties confirmed they would not enter the governing coalition.

