====Other notable defeats====

*'''[[Toshiro Kurosawa]]''' (PPO, Miyagi-3rd) — The former Prime Minister and architect of the Satori digital infrastructure programme lost his seat to '''[[Yuriko Katsumata]]''' (SCP), a former Defence Ministry analyst who had served under Ikazaki during the Aoyagi government. Katsumata won with 44.1% in a three-way race. Kurosawa's defeat was the most prominent individual result of election night and was widely interpreted as a symbolic repudiation of the digital governance project he had initiated during his own premiership. Katsumata was subsequently described by the ''Aizawa Mainichi'' as "Ikazaki's enforcer" — a characterization she has neither confirmed nor denied.

*'''[[Haruki Enomoto]]''' (PPO, Seto-1st) — The former Minister for Internal Affairs and Communications lost to SCP challenger Hara Tatsuya by 3,891 votes. Post-election reporting revealed that Enomoto had received the same internal ministry warnings about Satori's disaster recovery protocols that Onizuka presented at the second debate, and had forwarded them to the Chief Cabinet Secretary's office without comment. His defeat was described by the ''Toyozawa Shinbun'' as "the Satori verdict rendered at the ballot box."

*'''[[Seiran Mochizuki]]''' (OSP) won the [[Okami 6th District]] with 54.2% of the vote, defeating PPO incumbent [[Mamoru Hayashi]], who had held the seat since 2017. Mochizuki, the lead organiser of the OTWU during the March Maelstrom strike, had been dismissed by the PPO's Transport Ministry as "an agitator with no mandate." Her margin of victory was the largest of any OSP challenger in an urban Okami seat and was attributed in part to Nakayama personally campaigning in Okami-6th twice in the final week.

====Notable survivals====

*'''[[Honda Shigetsuma]]''' (PPO, Noto-1st) retained his seat with 41.2% of the vote, a reduction from his 2025 majority of 62.0%. His margin over the SCP challenger was 3,204 votes — the narrowest incumbency victory in the district's modern history. His survival was widely attributed to the separation voters in Noto made between their assessment of the Honda dynasty's local legacy and their verdict on his government's national record.

*'''[[Minoru Kihara]]''' (PPO, Aizawa-2nd), the former Chief Cabinet Secretary whose office had signed the directive overruling Satori disaster recovery warnings, survived with his seat intact — a result that drew comment given the fate of several cabinet colleagues. Kihara's survival was attributed to the fact that his direct role in the directive had not been publicly confirmed until Onizuka's second debate revelation, leaving insufficient time for the information to affect his local campaign before polling day.

*'''[[Satsuki Katayama]]''' (LDP, Ehime-1st), who had served as Minister of Finance in the PPO-led cabinet as a crossbench appointment, retained her seat comfortably — the LDP's general resilience in Ehime insulating her from the anti-PPO swing that swept out six of her cabinet colleagues.

===Notable wins===

*'''[[Honda Shigetsuma]]''' (PPO, Noto-1st) retained his seat with 41.2% of the vote — a victory, but a stark reduction from his 2025 majority of 62.0%. His margin over the SCP challenger was 3,204 votes, the narrowest incumbency victory in the district's modern history. Honda won despite, or perhaps because of, his resignation announcement — voters in Noto appeared to separate their affection for the Honda dynasty from their verdict on his government.

*'''[[Rento Nakayama]]''' (OSP, Okami-1st) won his district with 58% of the vote, down from 62% in 2025 but sufficient to confirm his mandate as party leader and incoming Deputy Prime Minister.

*'''[[Kousuke Ikazaki]]''' (SCP), having assumed the party leadership only twenty days before the election, stood on the Okami proportional representation list and did not contest a constituency seat. His decision to stand on the party list rather than an SMD was consistent with his role as national party leader and allowed the SCP to maximise its PR allocation in the Okami Block.

*'''[[Shimane Yoshida]]''' (SDP, Miyagi-1st) successfully defended the seat she had won in a 2022 by-election, increasing her majority to 44.1% — a result attributed almost entirely to the momentum generated by her second debate performance and the subsequent adoption of the Yoshida Threshold by all major parties.

*'''[[Soichiro Onizuka]]''' (NKP, Seto-3rd) successfully defended his seat. His document revelation during the second debate — exposing the Prime Minister's office directive overruling Satori safety warnings — was credited with driving the NKP's modest national seat gain and cementing his reputation as the parliament's most effective accountability advocate.

*'''[[Kei Yamada]]''' (OIP, Suzuka-2nd) successfully defended his seat despite the OIP's modest national performance, his personal vote in Suzuka proving resilient against both SCP and OSP challengers in the technology manufacturing constituency most directly affected by the Blackout.

*'''[[Hayashi Akihiro]]''' (Independent, Ogasawara-3rd) — a former maritime emergency coordinator who organized the civilian response on Ogasawara during the Satori Blackout, when the island chain was entirely cut off from mainland communications for 72 hours — won the [[Ogasawara 3rd District]], defeating LDP incumbent [[Tsuyoshi Fujita]]. Hayashi ran on a platform of remote community resilience and emergency infrastructure sovereignty, arguing that "the Blackout proved that Okaiken builds its safety systems for the capital and prays for the islands." His victory, with 41.2% of the vote in a two-way race, made him the first independent elected to the House of Commoners since 2008. He subsequently announced he would support the Ikazaki Cabinet in the House.

===Other notable new members===

*'''[[Yuriko Katsumata]]''' (SCP, Miyagi-3rd) — a former Defence Ministry analyst who served under Ikazaki during the Aoyagi government, defeated former Prime Minister [[Toshiro Kurosawa]] in the night's most prominent individual upset. Katsumata had been one of the analysts whose internal reports on Satori's defense vulnerabilities were excluded from the government's 2024 integration review.

*'''[[Seiran Mochizuki]]''' (OSP, Okami-6th) — lead OTWU organiser during the March Maelstrom strike, defeated PPO incumbent [[Mamoru Hayashi]], who had held the seat since 2017, with 54.2% of the vote. Her victory was the largest margin of any OSP challenger in an urban Okami seat.



==Coalition formation: The "Three-Day War"==
{{main|Ikazaki Cabinet}}

The election produced no majority — the SCP, OSP, and SDP combined held 251 seats, sufficient for a governing majority with 13 seats to spare. However, the formation of a government required three days of negotiation that became known in political commentary as the {{Nihongo|"Three-Day War"|三日間の戦争|Mikkakan no Sensō}}.

===Day 1 (May 17–18): The Mandate Claimed===
Ikazaki, whose SCP had won the most seats of any single party at 115, claimed by constitutional convention the first right to attempt to form a government. His statement, issued at 23:41 on election night, was twelve sentences long. The final sentence read: "The fortress is built. Now we decide who lives inside it."

He did not celebrate. He did not take questions. He returned to SCP headquarters and began reviewing the coalition arithmetic.

Nakayama, whose OSP had won 100 seats and whose personal vote share remained higher than Ikazaki's in "preferred Prime Minister" polling, refused to concede the premiership immediately. His statement, issued at 00:17 on May 18th, noted that the OSP had received more votes in absolute terms and that the left bloc's mandate belonged to the movement as a whole, not to any single party. It was a careful statement — not a rejection of coalition, but a refusal to accept Ikazaki's framing.

Honda, true to his word, resigned as PPO leader at 09:00 on May 18th. His statement was four sentences. He did not take questions either.

===Day 2 (May 18): The Reform Centre Gambit===
Nakayama's team floated what his office called a "Reform Centre" coalition: OSP (100) + SDP (36) + NKP (32) + OIP (18) + Independent (1) = 187 seats — 51 short of a majority but presented as a viable minority government. The calculation was transparently a negotiating position rather than a genuine proposal; both NKP leader Onizuka and OIP leader Yamada declined to publicly confirm participation within hours of the leak.

Ikazaki's response to the gambit was characteristically precise. He issued no public statement. He called Onizuka directly and spoke for eleven minutes. He called Yamada directly and spoke for eight minutes. Neither call has been fully characterized by either party, but both leaders subsequently confirmed they would not participate in a Nakayama-led minority government.

By the end of Day 2, the arithmetic had clarified into its true shape. The only viable majority government required both the SCP and the OSP. The only question was who would lead it.

===Day 3 (May 19): The 06:00 Call===
At 06:00 on May 19th, Nakayama called Ikazaki. The call lasted 47 minutes. Neither principal has characterized it beyond the briefest acknowledgment. What is known — from subsequent reporting by the ''Toyozawa Shinbun'', citing sources in both parties — is that Nakayama opened with a single question:

{{blockquote|"What do you need to make this work?"}}

Ikazaki's answer took thirty-eight of the forty-seven minutes. It was, by all accounts, a complete list — not a demand, not a negotiating position, but the final version of an arrangement he had prepared in advance of the call. When he finished, Nakayama was silent for approximately twelve seconds. Then he said: "Agreed."

The remaining seven minutes concerned scheduling.

What Ikazaki had said, in those thirty-eight minutes, was the following: that he would serve as Prime Minister; that Nakayama would serve as Deputy Prime Minister with a social affairs portfolio that gave him genuine authority over the Two-Man Mandate implementation and the welfare expansion the OSP had campaigned on; that the SCP's Justice appointment would pursue criminal charges against Kizan Systems under the Critical Infrastructure Protection Act; that the Yoshida Threshold — 40% women in cabinet — would be a binding coalition condition, not an aspiration; and that the Yamamoto Hina Safety Commission would be established by executive order on Day 1 of the new government.

"The title comes with the building," Ikazaki told a single aide afterward, when asked to characterize the call. "That was always the arrangement."

===The Final Deal===
The coalition agreement, signed at 17:00 on May 19th, established the following:

* '''Prime Minister:''' Kousuke Ikazaki (SCP)
* '''Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Social Affairs:''' Rento Nakayama (OSP)
* '''Minister of Finance:''' SCP appointment
* '''Minister of Defence:''' SCP appointment
* '''Minister of Justice:''' SCP appointment — responsible for the Kizan Systems prosecution
* '''Minister of Health and Welfare:''' OSP appointment
* '''Minister of Transport:''' OSP appointment — responsible for Two-Man Mandate implementation
* '''The Yoshida Threshold:''' A formal coalition condition insisted upon by the SDP as the price of their support, requiring that the cabinet contain at minimum 40% women — the first such formal commitment in Sanese political history. SDP leader [[Shimane Yoshida]] accepted a ministerial portfolio overseeing gender equality and democratic accountability.
* '''The Yamamoto Hina Safety Commission:''' Established by executive order on Day 1 of the new government, named per Ikazaki's promise to Yamamoto Kenji during the first debate, with statutory authority to ground any rail service without ministerial approval.
* '''Kizan Systems prosecution:''' The Ministry of Justice empowered to pursue criminal charges under the Critical Infrastructure Protection Act within the first 30 days.
* '''Two-Man Mandate:''' Committed to as a legislative priority within the first 90 days.
* '''Digital Sovereignty Act:''' The SDP's flagship legislation, committed to within the first 100 days.

The [[Ikazaki Cabinet]] was formally sworn in on May 20, 2026. Ikazaki arrived at the swearing-in ceremony eleven minutes early. He was the only principal who did so.

===Reactions===
The formation of the Ikazaki Cabinet was received with a combination of alarm, relief, and a quality of national attention that political commentators struggled to categorize. The ''Okami Times'' editorial board wrote: "Okaiken has elected governments before. It has not previously elected one that makes you feel the temperature of the room has changed."

Nakayama, in his first statement as Deputy Prime Minister, said: "We did not agree on everything. We agreed on what matters. That is what governing requires." He was asked by a reporter whether he had expected to end the negotiation as Deputy Prime Minister rather than Prime Minister. He paused for a moment before answering: "I expected to end it with a government that could do the work. That is what we have."

Shimane Yoshida, whose SDP support had proved the coalition's deciding margin, issued a statement notable for its brevity: "The Yoshida Threshold is now law. The rest is implementation."

Honda Shigetsuma, reached by the ''Aizawa Mainichi'' for comment, said nothing that was published. His office confirmed he had watched the swearing-in ceremony from his constituency office in Noto.

Sadanuma, leading the LDP as the largest opposition bloc at 110 seats, confirmed the party would serve as a "responsible opposition" — a phrase he used three times in his statement, which political analysts interpreted as a signal that the LDP would not obstruct the Kizan prosecution despite the party's historical ties to the corporate infrastructure sector.

Hayashi Akihiro, the independent member for Ogasawara-3rd, announced his support for the coalition government in a statement that contained one sentence: "The islands were forgotten once. The Digital Sovereignty Act ensures it cannot happen again."

==Historical significance==
The 2026 election is widely regarded by political historians as the most consequential in modern Sanese history since the SCP's [[Okaiken general election, 2016|2016 victory]] under [[Sho Aoyagi]]. The collapse of the PPO — from 181 seats to 63 in a single election — ended the party's dominance of Sanese politics after it had taken over in 2021. The combined OSP-SCP bloc's 215 seats represented the largest left-wing parliamentary majority since the Aoyagi government, though unlike 2016, the majority was formed without PPO participation.

The election also marked several institutional firsts: the first cabinet with a formal gender composition threshold; the first criminal prosecution of a private contractor for infrastructure failures; the first independent member elected to the House of Commoners since 2008; and the first establishment of a rail safety commission with independent grounding authority.

Political scientist {{nihongo|Yamamoto Keiko|山本恵子}} of [[Okami University]] wrote in her post-election analysis: "The 2026 election did not simply produce a new government. It produced a renegotiation of the terms under which government in Okaiken operates. The Yoshida Threshold, the Yamamoto Hina Commission, the Kizan prosecution — these are not policy outcomes. They are constitutional settlements, arrived at through the specific combination of crisis, accountability, and democratic pressure that the election campaign generated."

